WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.077
Format selection is a security decision because formats make different
behavior easy.

2
00:00:05.077 --> 00:00:08.644
JSON is widely used for APIs and interoperable data exchange.

3
00:00:08.644 --> 00:00:11.800
YAML is often used for human-readable configuration.

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.367
XML appears in document-oriented systems and integrations.

5
00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:17.425
CSV is common for tabular exchange.

6
00:00:17.425 --> 00:00:22.365
Binary and schema-based formats can support compact messages and strong
contracts.

7
00:00:22.365 --> 00:00:29.637
Object serialization formats can preserve Python-specific structures, but
they are usually a poor fit for untrusted input.

8
00:00:29.637 --> 00:00:34.645
Safer design often favors simple data formats over automatic object
reconstruction.

9
00:00:34.645 --> 00:00:43.289
A plain data format encourages the application to parse values, validate a
schema, and explicitly decide how those values map into program behavior.

10
00:00:43.289 --> 00:00:50.972
Object serialization can blur that boundary by combining input data with
class loading, object construction, and hidden behavior.

11
00:00:50.972 --> 00:00:57.421
That may be acceptable for controlled internal persistence, but it should
not be the default for external data.

12
00:00:57.421 --> 00:00:59.068
Every format has tradeoffs.

13
00:00:59.068 --> 00:01:01.949
Readability helps humans review configuration.

14
00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:05.379
Interoperability helps different systems exchange data.

15
00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.232
Strictness reduces ambiguity.

16
00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:09.564
Performance matters for large systems.

17
00:01:09.564 --> 00:01:11.759
Schema support improves validation.

18
00:01:11.759 --> 00:01:17.865
Security depends on safe parser settings, bounded input, minimal features,
and explicit expectations.

19
00:01:17.865 --> 00:01:24.931
There is no universally perfect format; the right choice depends on source
trust, use case, and operational constraints.

20
00:01:24.931 --> 00:01:28.842
When in doubt, choose the least powerful format that meets the need.

21
00:01:28.842 --> 00:01:36.182
If the application only needs records or configuration values, do not
choose a format that reconstructs application objects.

22
00:01:36.182 --> 00:01:41.190
If the application only needs a narrow set of fields, reject broad dynamic
structures.

23
00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:49.560
Format choice should make the safe path easy: parse ordinary data,
validate it, normalize it, authorize its use, and handle errors
predictably.
